WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:06.609
Hello, and thank you for joining us for this
NCBI Minute.

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:11.720
My name is Sherri Holland Bailey from NCBI
customer services.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.400
We will learn how to Tailor Your PubMed search
experience using My NCBI.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.760
We begin by highlighting search terms making
it easier to scan results, customize search

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.260
filters to narrow results, and conclude by
permanently displaying up to 200 records per

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.670
page.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.759
Let's get started with highlighting.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:38.550
For today's example I am using my free, newly
created NCBI account that can be accessed

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.220
directly from PubMed.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:47.780
If you are already logged into your NCBI account,
you can return to the My NCBI home page by

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:54.980
clicking on the My NCBI link located beside
your username on the top right.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:01.870
You may wish to access your NCBI account directly
with the following URL (www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/account).

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:08.320
Now that I am logged in to my My NCBI homepage,
I will click the NCBI site preferences beneath

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.110
the blue toolbar and to the right.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:18.900
Beneath common preferences, click highlighting
and a pop-up window will allow you to select

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.840
your favorite highlighting color.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:22.840
Click save.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:29.450
A confirmation of changes can be seen both
here and here.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.950
Let's return to PubMed where I have created
a search for asthma.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:38.869
Notice the term asthma is highlighted in number
four because it appears in the title.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:47.090
Although my search term does not appear in
1, 2 or 3, my term does appear in keywords

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.780
and multiple times in the abstract.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:56.130
This allows me to quickly scan and determine
if this article could fit my research needs.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.939
Let's set our filters.

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:03.560
Filters assist in narrowing down search results
to smaller groups of data in the NCBI databases.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:08.319
For example, I am doing extensive research
for the most recent publication dealing with

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:12.809
adolescent chronic obstructive pulmonary disease
or COPD.

00:02:12.809 --> 00:02:18.209
I use filters to sort through the thousands
of citations in PubMed to target at the most

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:20.879
relevant results for my research.

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:23.609
A quick word about filters.

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:28.780
You are allowed to use the maximum of 15 filters
within PubMed.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:34.609
To get started, you may wish to select from
aCtegory and your choices include: Popular,

00:02:34.609 --> 00:02:43.879
which is most common; LinkOut, which groups
resources provided by outside organizations;

00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:49.760
Properties, which groups records according
to specific criteria such as age, human or

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:57.590
animal and male or female; and finally, Links,
which links to other NCBI databases.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:03.290
At your convenience, I encourage you to fully
explore the predefined PubMed filters.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:08.799
For the second time, I have selected a few
filters from Categories.

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:15.819
Once selected, the filters will appear in
your PubMed filtered list column.

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:21.930
Any filter you wish to be active requires
you to be logged into your NCBI account and

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:27.689
will be active as long as the box is checked.

00:03:27.689 --> 00:03:31.089
I'm interested in adolescent COPD.

00:03:31.089 --> 00:03:35.919
Although standard filters are useful they
do not always capture every filtering need.

00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:39.180
This is a job for custom filters.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.829
Once you click Create Custom filter a pop-up
window will appear.

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:48.739
Here is where you will enter the term or terms
to create the customized filter.

00:03:48.739 --> 00:03:55.959
In my example, I entered terms that will locate
items referring to COPD, adolescents published

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:58.369
in the year 2017.

00:03:58.369 --> 00:04:00.639
You may have noticed the bracketed DP.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:04.659
DP is a PubMed search field description tag.

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:10.239
For those new to description tag or need a
refresher, a great tutorial is linked at the

00:04:10.239 --> 00:04:11.530
bottom of the screen.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:12.530
(www.nlm.nih.gov/bsd/disted/pubmedtutorial/020_710.html).

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:18.229
You will also be able to find it in additional
materials posted at the end of this presentation.

00:04:18.229 --> 00:04:20.549
Next I will test my query.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:25.870
Based on the initial results, I may decide
to further broaden or narrow it.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.789
Now it is time to create a name for the filter.

00:04:27.789 --> 00:04:35.490
I have taken the guesswork out of what a filter
does by naming it COPD Adolescent 2017.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:39.570
Once complete, click on save filter.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.750
Custom filters will not automatically be active.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.669
They must be selected.

00:04:45.669 --> 00:04:50.920
Custom filters may be removed by clicking
the delete link.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:57.400
Predefined filters may be removed by unchecking
the appropriate box in the Active column.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:04.169
You may also edit custom filters at any time
by clicking the gear, modifying your query,

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:07.720
and clicking save filter.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.560
Now that I have all of my filters in place,
let's take a look at how it appears in PubMed.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.879
Notice I still have my search term asthma.

00:05:15.879 --> 00:05:20.139
Customized filters are on the right.

00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:26.240
In the parentheses, total number of items
available is shown based on my search of asthma,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.539
which is seen in All.

00:05:29.539 --> 00:05:35.729
If I'm interested in only Free full text,
I will click the corresponding link.

00:05:35.729 --> 00:05:41.290
Notice, you can quickly manage your filters
by clicking on the manage filters link which

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:46.580
will re-direct you to the filters page in
My NCBI.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:53.259
Now that we have created filters, we are also
able to quickly activate, deactivate, modify

00:05:53.259 --> 00:06:00.490
and manage filters from the My NCBI homepage
without the extra step of My NCBI site preferences.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.310
A quick recap.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:11.919
So far we have learned how to highlight search
terms, create, modify, activate and use filters.

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:18.389
And now we will learn to permanently display
up to 200 records in a PubMed page.

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:23.639
Once you click Results Display Settings, a
pop-up window will appear.

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:29.550
Select the desired number of results per page
and click save.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.139
Now that the results have been saved, let's
see it in action.

00:06:33.139 --> 00:06:37.699
I can verify the amount of items per page
in two places.

00:06:37.699 --> 00:06:41.199
Beneath search results and beside per page.

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:47.810
You may also temporarily change the default
by using the drop-down box next to per page.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:53.069
These are just a few of the ways to tailor
your PubMed experience.

00:06:53.069 --> 00:06:59.840
Thank you for joining us and please feel free
to ask any remaining questions at this time.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:00.889
Thank you.

00:07:00.889 --> 00:07:03.969
I don't see any questions that we haven't
already answered in the chat pod.

00:07:03.969 --> 00:07:08.300
Let's go on to the next slide, which has the
help document links on it.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:16.289
Here is a slide that has more information
and URLs that will give you more help using

00:07:16.289 --> 00:07:19.590
PubMed search field tags and other things
about My NCBI.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:27.810
Check out our blog at, ncbiinsights.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov,
and make sure you check out our YouTube channel.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:31.740
Remember that you can write to us at the helpdesk,
info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:36.360
Thanks everybody for coming and we will see
you next time.

